# A program that the dotnet host never starts (here: its runtimeconfig.json
# asks for Microsoft.NETCore.App 99.0.0, which is not installed) is a
# configuration error of msil-run.py, exit status 2 with a 'could not start'
# message, never an '[exit N]' transcript. The host fails with the HRESULT
# 0x80008096; POSIX keeps only its low 8 bits, so on Linux (the WSL leg) a
# harness that took that status for the program's would print '[exit 150]'
# and accept Inputs/host-startup-150.expected, and on Windows it would report
# a mismatch (status 1). The --runtimeconfig given here comes after the one in
# %msil_exec and %msil_jitall, so it wins. msil-jitall.py treats a jitall that
# the host does not start the same way.

# RUN: %ilasm -dll -output=%t.dll %S/Inputs/exit-42.il
# RUN: %{harness_check} exits 2 %msil_exec --runtimeconfig %S/Inputs/net99.runtimeconfig.json --expect %S/Inputs/host-startup-150 %t.dll > %t.exec.log 2>&1
# RUN: %{harness_check} contains %t.exec.log "could not start"
# RUN: %{harness_check} lacks %t.exec.log "^\[exit"

# RUN: %if msil-jitall %{ %{harness_check} exits 2 %msil_jitall --runtimeconfig %S/Inputs/net99.runtimeconfig.json %t.dll > %t.jitall.log 2>&1 %}
# RUN: %if msil-jitall %{ %{harness_check} contains %t.jitall.log "could not start" %}

# The control: the same program with the runtimeconfig of %msil_exec runs.
# RUN: %msil_exec --expect %S/Inputs/exit-42 %t.dll
